# No ESLint Disable Comments

**Never use `eslint-disable` comments to solve linting issues.** Fix the underlying problem instead.

## Core Principle

ESLint rules exist to enforce code quality and prevent bugs. Disabling them hides problems rather than solving them.

## ❌ BAD: Using Disable Comments

```typescript
// ❌ BAD: Disabling the rule instead of fixing the issue
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
function processData(data: any) {
  return data.value
}

// ❌ BAD: Disabling for entire file
/* eslint-disable @typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars */
```

## ✅ GOOD: Fix the Underlying Issue

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Fix the type issue
function processData(data: { value: string }) {
  return data.value
}

// ✅ GOOD: Remove unused variable or use it
function calculateTotal(items: Item[]) {
  return items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0)
}
```

## Common Patterns

### Type Safety Issues

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
function handleEvent(event: any) {}

// ✅ GOOD: Define proper type
interface CustomEvent {
  type: string
  payload: unknown
}
function handleEvent(event: CustomEvent) {}
```

### Unused Variables

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-unused-vars
function processItems(items: Item[]) {
  return items.length
}

// ✅ GOOD: Remove unused parameter or prefix with underscore
function processItems(_items: Item[]) {
  return 0
}

// ✅ GOOD: Or use the parameter
function processItems(items: Item[]) {
  console.log(`Processing ${items.length} items`)
  return items.length
}
```

### Console Statements

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
// eslint-disable-next-line no-console
console.log('Debug info')

// ✅ GOOD: Use proper logging or remove
import { logger } from '@/lib/logger'
logger.debug('Debug info')

// ✅ GOOD: Or remove if not needed
```

### Async/Await Issues

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-floating-promises
someAsyncFunction()

// ✅ GOOD: Handle the promise properly
await someAsyncFunction()

// ✅ GOOD: Or handle errors explicitly
someAsyncFunction().catch((error) => {
  logger.error('Failed to execute', { error })
})
```

## When Disable Comments Are Acceptable

Disable comments are **only acceptable** in these rare cases:

1. **Generated code** - Files that are auto-generated and shouldn't be manually edited
2. **Third-party type definitions** - When working with external library types
3. **Legacy code migration** - Temporarily during refactoring (must include TODO to fix)

```typescript
// ✅ ACCEPTABLE: Generated file
/* eslint-disable */
// This file is auto-generated by Convex
// Do not edit manually

// ✅ ACCEPTABLE: Temporary during migration (with TODO)
// TODO: Refactor to remove any types
// eslint-disable-next-line @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any
function legacyFunction(data: any) {}
```

## Action Items

When encountering an ESLint error:

1. **Read the error message** - Understand what the rule is checking
2. **Fix the root cause** - Address the actual issue, not the symptom
3. **Consider if the rule is wrong** - If the rule is incorrect for your use case, update the ESLint config instead of disabling
4. **Ask for help** - If unsure how to fix, discuss the approach rather than disabling

## Rule Configuration

If a rule is consistently problematic across the codebase, **update the ESLint configuration** rather than disabling it everywhere:

```javascript
// ✅ GOOD: Update eslint.config.js
export default {
  rules: {
    '@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any': 'warn', // Downgrade to warning
    // or
    '@typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any': 'off', // Disable globally if truly needed
  },
}
```
